  - [1mHostname detection[0m — settings module maps [38;5;153mwindow.location.hostname[39m to an endpoint. Works with the static files you
    have today, one file to read to see every environment, no deploy step. Costs: environments must be distinguishable
    by hostname, and all endpoints are visible in the shipped source.
  - [1mDeploy-time file swap[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m holds one environment's values; the deploy copies the right variant over it.
    Dead simple, no logic. Costs: needs a deploy process to be correct, and "which environment am I on" isn't answerable
    from the source alone.
  - [1mBuild-time injection[0m — add a bundler/env-substitution step and read config from an env var. The standard answer at
    scale, and the only one that keeps non-production endpoints out of the shipped bundle. Costs: introduces a whole
    toolchain this repo currently doesn't have, for one string.
  - [1mRuntime injected global[0m — deploy writes a [38;5;153mwindow.APP_CONFIG[39m script tag before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m; the settings module reads it
    with a fallback. Keeps one built artifact across all environments. Costs: config lives partly outside the repo, and
    you need the fallback path for local dev.

  [1mMy recommendation: hostname detection.[0m It's the only option that changes environments with zero new tooling and zero
  deploy choreography, which is what "easier to change environments" most likely means for a static page with one
  endpoint. Build-time injection is the right answer once you have real secrets or a real build — and moving to it later
  is cheap, because every option here ends with [38;5;153mapp.js[39m reading [38;5;153msettings.apiEndpoint[39m instead of a literal, so the
  consumer doesn't change.
